        <h3 class="cag-h3">Ready-Made Pair Versus Two Singles You Introduce</h3>
        <p>A ready-made pair is easier on arrival and harder afterwards. Two singles are harder on arrival — you run the introduction yourself — and easier afterwards, because neither bird arrives already committed to the other.</p>
        <h3 class="cag-h3">Why a Proven Breeding Pair Is a Different Purchase Entirely</h3>
        <p><a href="/african-grey-breeding-pair-for-sale/">Aviary stock valued on its breeding record</a> is priced by what it produces rather than by how it behaves on a hand, and most of it is not hand-tame at all. If someone offers you a proven breeding pair as a family companion purchase, they are selling you the wrong animal.</p>
        <h4 class="cag-h4">Which Route Suits a First-Time Grey Owner?</h4>
        <p>None of them, honestly. A first-time owner is best served by a single hand-raised bird, and we would rather say so here than after your deposit clears.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>
      <!-- ═══ §11 THE ARITHMETIC ═══ -->
      <section id="arithmetic">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">Does Buying a Pair Cost Less Than Buying Two Single Greys?</h2>
        <p>Slightly, and we would rather show you the arithmetic than let it look like a bulk discount on animals.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">What Two Singles Come To at Our Prices</h3>
        <p>Jins on his own is $1,900 and Jeni on her own is $1,800, so taking both separately would come to $3,700. Together they are $3,500. The $200 difference is not a volume discount — it is the handling and administration that genuinely only happens once.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">What Changes on the Second Bird, and What Does Not</h3>
        <p>What happens once: the reservation, the crate booking, the flight arrangement, the conversation with you. What happens twice, at full cost: DNA sexing, the avian vet examination, the disease panel, the CITES documentation, and sixteen weeks of hand-feeding and socialisation.</p>
        <h4 class="cag-h4">Do You Pay Shipping Twice for Two Birds?</h4>
        <p>No. Airport pickup stays $185 and home delivery stays $350 for the reservation rather than per bird.</p>
        <h5 class="cag-h5">One Reservation, One Crate Booking, Two Sets of Papers</h5>
        <p>That sentence is the entire economics of a pair, and any seller offering a much steeper pair discount is discounting something else.</p>
        <h6 class="cag-h6"><span class="h6k">From the Aviary:</span> What the Second Bird Adds to a Booking, and What It Does Not</h6>
        <p class="note">A second bird adds paperwork, screening and rearing time. It does not add a second flight, a second crate fee, or a second round of our time on the phone with you.</p>
      </section>

      <div class="seam-wrap" aria-hidden="true"><img class="seam" src={seam} alt="" width="182" height="60" loading="lazy" decoding="async" /></div>

      <!-- ═══ §12 MARKET COMPARISON — both brackets ═══ -->
      <section id="market">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">What Does a Documented Congo Pair Cost on the Open Market?</h2>
        <p>Pair pricing is the widest spread in the whole African Grey market, and the spread itself is informative. Our $3,500 sits between two very different kinds of listing.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">$6,500 to $8,000 Is the Going Rate for a Documented Adult Pair</h3>
        <p>Browse the classified boards and documented adult Congo pairs are asking between $6,500 and $8,000 — a DNA-tested pair in Georgia at $8,000, bonded pairs in Maryland and Illinois at $6,500 each. Those are adult, settled, often proven birds, and the prices are real.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">Why Some Listings Show a Pair at $1,800</h3>
        <p>At the other end, retail sites advertise African Grey pairs around $1,800, frequently with no breeder name, no licence number, no certificates and an add-to-cart button. Those two figures are not measuring the same thing.</p>
        <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={`${IMG}/african-grey-pair-price-bracket.webp`} width="1376" height="768" style={"aspect-ratio:1376/768"}
               alt="Where our $3,500 documented pair sits between $1,800 undocumented listings and $8,000 adult pairs"
               loading="lazy" decoding="async"  srcset={`${String(`${IMG}/african-grey-pair-price-bracket.webp`).replace(/(-\d{2,4})?(\.[a-zA-Z0-9]+)$/, '-480$2')} 480w, ${`${IMG}/african-grey-pair-price-bracket.webp`} 1376w`} sizes="(max-width:1024px) 100vw, 59.4vw" />
          <figcaption class="img-note">The spread itself is the information. What the price sits next to tells you more than the price.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h4 class="cag-h4">Is a Cheaper Pair Always a Scam?</h4>
        <p>No, and this is where most anti-scam advice gets lazy. Price alone tells you very little. What the price sits next to tells you everything.</p>
        <h5 class="cag-h5">A Low Price With Papers Is Value; A Low Price Without Them Is Bait</h5>
        <p>Ask for the certificates, the licence number and a live video call with both birds. A legitimate seller under market has a reason. A fraudulent one has an excuse.</p>
        <h6 class="cag-h6"><span class="h6k">Before You Commit:</span> We Breed Ours, So No Reseller Margin Sits in the Price</h6>
        <p class="note">Jins and Jeni hatched here and were hand-fed here. There is no importer, no broker and no middleman between our aviary and your house, and that gap is the whole reason our pair costs roughly half what a comparable documented pair asks elsewhere.</p>

        <p class="ctaline"><a class="btn-clay" href="#reserve">See What Each Pair Includes →</a></p>
      </section>

