      <!-- ═══ §20 VERIFY ═══ -->
      <section id="verify">
        <h2 class="cag-h2">How Do You Verify Anyone Selling You an African Grey Pair?</h2>
        <p>Pair listings attract fraud because the ticket is bigger. These are the checks we would run on ourselves, and they take about ten minutes.</p>

        <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
          <img class="sec-img og-photo og-scene" src={`${IMG}/our-greys-handled-at-home-midland.webp`} width="1408" height="768"
               alt="A grey out of its cage in our living room with a family member close by, the kind of unstaged proof any seller should be able to film"
               loading="lazy" decoding="async"  srcset={`${String(`${IMG}/our-greys-handled-at-home-midland.webp`).replace(/(-\d{2,4})?(\.[a-zA-Z0-9]+)$/, '-460$2')} 460w, ${`${IMG}/our-greys-handled-at-home-midland.webp`} 1408w`} sizes="(max-width:1024px) 92.1vw, 59.4vw" />
          <figcaption class="img-note">Ask any seller for a live video call with both birds. We will do one on request.</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">Five Checks That Take Under Ten Minutes</h3>
        <p>Ask for both birds' DNA certificates by name. Ask for the breeder's USDA licence number. Ask for a live video call with both birds in frame. Ask what happens if you only want one. Ask the hatch dates, then check them against whatever the listing claims about the birds' status.</p>

        <h3 class="cag-h3">The Claims That Should End the Conversation</h3>
        <p><a href="/how-to-avoid-african-grey-parrot-scams/">Our full scam-pattern breakdown</a> covers the rest, but three claims are immediately disqualifying on a pair listing: a bonded pair of four-month-olds, a breeding pair of yearlings, and worldwide shipping inside a few days. ↗</p>
        <figure class="sec-img-wrap">
          <img class="sec-img inf-img" src={`${IMG}/verify-african-grey-pair-seller-checks.webp`} width="1376" height="768" style={"aspect-ratio:1376/768"}
               alt="Five verification questions to ask any African Grey pair seller, and the three answers that end it"
               loading="lazy" decoding="async"  srcset={`${String(`${IMG}/verify-african-grey-pair-seller-checks.webp`).replace(/(-\d{2,4})?(\.[a-zA-Z0-9]+)$/, '-480$2')} 480w, ${`${IMG}/verify-african-grey-pair-seller-checks.webp`} 1376w`} sizes="(max-width:1024px) 100vw, 59.4vw" />
          <figcaption class="img-note">Ten minutes of questions. Any seller who cannot answer these is telling you something.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <h4 class="cag-h4">Why Does "Bonded Pair, Four Months Old" Make No Sense?</h4>
        <p>Because a four-month-old grey is barely weaned. It can have a companion. It cannot have a pair bond in any sense that survives contact with the word.</p>
        <h5 class="cag-h5">Greys Reach Sexual Maturity at Three to Five Years, Never Months</h5>
        <p>That single fact disqualifies a large share of the pair listings currently ranking for these searches.</p>
        <h6 class="cag-h6"><span class="h6k">From the Aviary:</span> A Pair Advertised as Breeding at Fifteen Months Cannot Breed</h6>
        <p class="note">We have watched that exact listing rank first for breeding-pair searches. Whatever those birds are, they are not a breeding pair, and the seller either does not know or is counting on you not to.</p>

        <p class="ctaline"><a class="btn-clay" href="#reserve">Send Us Your Verification Questions →</a></p>
      </section>

